# 1. Executive Summary

The primary problem is excessive Firestore document reads across the application. The project reportedly exhausted its approximately 50,000 daily free-tier document-read allowance, after which users—including administrators—could no longer log in or use the application normally.

The requested objective is to redesign data access so that the application can operate reliably within free-tier limits without enabling paid billing.

The affected areas include:

- Student portal and examinations.
- Tutor portal and assessment management.
- Parent dashboard.
- School administration dashboard.
- Staff dashboard.
- Main administration dashboard.
- Netlify serverless functions.
- Firebase client and Admin SDK configuration.
- Two Firestore databases in one Firebase project: `(default)` and a named Enterprise database created through the AI Studio workflow.
- Existing database-copying/synchronisation logic.
- Administrative visibility into daily database reads.

The audit branch contains a first round of read-reduction changes, examination autosave improvements, bounded queries, an authenticated server endpoint for Firestore usage metrics, and an administrative usage panel.

**This is not yet a completed repository-wide audit or a finished database failover implementation.** The work has not been deployed, and two CI workflows currently fail at dependency installation. Google AI Studio should continue from the existing branch rather than assume the current implementation is production-ready.

The guiding principle for the remaining work is:

> Reduce the number of reads generated by each user action, make queries specific to the requesting user and their permissions, avoid repeated reads of unchanged data, and design database failover as a coordinated server-and-client operation with explicit consistency rules—not merely a browser-side database switch.

